p1_say = [
    "In traditional software engineering libraries, Semantic Versioning (SemVer) follows a well-understood, mathematical contract: given a version number formatted as MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, increment PATCH for backwards-compatible bug fixes; increment MINOR for backwards-compatible new features; and increment MAJOR for incompatible, breaking API modifications. For a standard utility library like lodash or date-fns, identifying a breaking change is straightforward: if a function signature alters its parameter types, returns a different data structure, or throws an unhandled exception, that change constitutes a breaking change requiring a major version bump. If the function continues to accept existing arguments and returns the expected result, it is backwards compatible.",
    "However, in the multidisciplinary world of enterprise design systems, Semantic Versioning becomes exponentially more nuanced, delicate, and multidimensional. A design system is not merely a collection of JavaScript functions; it is a holistic visual, spatial, and accessibility contract. In a design system, a breaking change can occur completely independently of TypeScript compiler errors. Consider a Button component where the TypeScript interface remains identical across versions: it still accepts variant, size, and onClick. But in the component stylesheet, the design team reduces the button's vertical padding from sixteen pixels to eight pixels, or changes its display property from inline-block to flex. While the TypeScript compiler compiles consumer code with zero errors and unit tests pass with flying colors, in production, consumer page layouts across hundreds of enterprise dashboards shift abruptly, wrapping text unexpectedly, clipping button labels, and breaking pixel-sensitive trading grids. In a design system, layout shifts are breaking changes.",
    "Furthermore, design system breaking changes encompass accessibility contracts and DOM structure. If a developer refactors an internal component from an HTML button element to a stylized div tag adorned with role='button', or if the component alters its internal tabIndex sequence or aria-expanded wiring, any consumer application that relied on standard keyboard tab order or native form submit bubbling is immediately broken. Similarly, in multi-brand token systems, modifying the default value of a global token like --pinit-color-text-primary from solid black to soft gray can cause thousands of consumer pages to violate legal WCAG contrast compliance overnight. Design systems must therefore define clear, institutional criteria for what constitutes a breaking change across three distinct layers: the API contract, the visual layout contract, and the accessibility contract.",
    "To manage this complexity without triggering perpetual major version fatigue, enterprise teams adopt atomic versioning policies backed by modern monorepo tooling such as Changesets. Instead of a single lead engineer attempting to manually deduce what version bump is required at release time by combing through hundreds of git commits, every developer who touches a component is required to generate an atomic changeset file on their pull request. This markdown file explicitly declares which specific packages are affected, whether the change is a patch, minor, or major increment, and provides a clear human-readable explanation of the change and its migration impact.",
    "When the pull request merges into the primary development branch, the automated release bot aggregates all accumulated changesets. It analyzes the dependency graph between packages, automatically bumps version numbers across package.json files, resolves inter-package dependencies (such as updating @pinit/components to depend on the newly bumped @pinit/tokens), and generates rich, automated CHANGELOG.md files detailing exact changes. This structured rigor eliminates human error and establishes a predictable cadence of continuous, safe evolution for the enterprise.",
    "To formalize these contracts, the design systems core team publishes an Enterprise SemVer Policy Document that serves as an engineering constitution. Under this policy, any mutation that alters an existing component's bounding box by more than one pixel, changes its default focus ring appearance, removes an exported TypeScript symbol, or inverts a color token's luminance ratio is classified as an automatic major breaking change. Conversely, purely internal code refactors that preserve pixel geometry and DOM semantics are certified as patch releases. Clear definitions empower developers to contribute enhancements with absolute confidence."
]

p2_say = [
    "In a modern enterprise engineering environment, design systems rarely exist as a single monolithic repository with one solitary package.json file. Instead, design systems are architected as modular multi-package monorepos containing dozens of interconnected packages: @pinit/tokens (raw design token definitions and DTCG compilation scripts), @pinit/icons (SVG icon primitives and tree-shakeable React components), @pinit/primitives (headless accessible hooks and unstyled UI state machines), @pinit/components (styled, brand-ready React/Vue/Web Component libraries), @pinit/eslint-plugin (automated static analysis rules and codemods), and @pinit/docs (interactive Storybook documentation and design portals).",
    "Managing releases manually across this interconnected web of dependencies is a recipe for catastrophic version drift. If an engineer updates a token in @pinit/tokens and bumps its version to 2.1.0, but forgets to update the dependency range in @pinit/components, consumer applications installing the components will receive stale token definitions or run into dependency resolution conflicts in pnpm, Yarn, or npm package managers. What is required is an automated, decentralized release management architecture.",
    "The industry standard solution for multi-package monorepo release orchestration is Changesets. The Changesets architecture decentralizes the responsibility of release documentation to the moment of pull request authoring. When a developer submits a pull request that alters code in one or more packages, a GitHub Action continuous integration check executes npx changeset status --since=origin/main. If the pull request modifies package code but does not include a corresponding .changeset/commit-id.md file, the continuous integration check fails with a clear prompt directing the author to run npx changeset.",
    "Running the changeset command launches an interactive terminal prompt that asks the developer two simple questions: Which packages should be released with this change? And for each package, is this change a patch, minor, or major version increment? The developer selects the affected packages, specifies the bump level, and types a concise summary of the change. Changesets writes this intent into a small YAML-frontmatter markdown file inside the .changeset directory and commits it directly to the feature branch. Because changesets are pure text files, they can be merged concurrently across dozens of independent feature branches without causing git merge conflicts.",
    "When pull requests merge into the main branch, a specialized Version Packages GitHub Action aggregates all accumulated changesets. It parses the dependency tree, increments version numbers across package.json files, updates internal workspace dependency ranges (such as workspace:^2.0.0), consumes and deletes the changeset files, and creates an automated Version Packages pull request. Once team leads review and merge this release PR, an automated publishing action runs pnpm publish --recursive, releasing all updated packages to the enterprise private npm registry with verified cryptographic provenance and zero human intervention.",
    "Crucially, Changesets supports linked packages and fixed versioning pools. In high-stakes financial platforms, design system core architects can configure @pinit/components and @pinit/tokens to increment in lockstep synchronization. If a major breaking change occurs in tokens, the coordination engine automatically bumps the components package to the matching major version number, eliminating confusing cross-package version mismatches across distributed engineering teams.",
    "Furthermore, the Changesets GitHub bot continuously monitors open pull requests and posts real-time preview comments. Reviewers can immediately see which packages will be published and read the generated changelog text before merging the code. By making release implications transparent during code review rather than at deployment time, the monorepo release pipeline prevents accidental major version bumps from slipping into production unnoticed.",
    "In large enterprise development organizations, release gating also incorporates canary and pre-release channels. By running npx changeset pre enter alpha, developers can publish experimental versions like 2.0.0-alpha.1 to internal npm registries. Feature squads can test bleeding-edge component primitives in staging environments without triggering production version bumps. Once feedback is gathered and edge cases resolved, npx changeset pre exit finalizes the release sequence, ensuring that published public releases are rock solid."
]

p3_say = [
    "When an enterprise design system releases a major version containing architectural breaking changes, the biggest barrier to adoption is developer migration fatigue. Imagine an enterprise with three thousand frontend engineers maintaining four hundred distinct web applications. If migrating to the new major version requires manually opening twenty thousand files to rename a prop from isPrimary={true} to variant='primary', and destructuring children inside Modal into Modal.Header and Modal.Body, the migration will take six months to two years to complete. During that prolonged transition period, technical debt multiplies, security patches must be backported across multiple major branches, and developer morale plummets.",
    "To eliminate this friction, world-class design systems deliver automated codemods alongside every major release. A codemod (code modification) is an automated script that parses source code files into Abstract Syntax Trees, programmatically transforms AST nodes according to strict algorithmic rules, and formats the modified AST back into clean, formatted source code with identical indentation and whitespace. The industry standard framework for authoring JavaScript and TypeScript codemods is jscodeshift, developed by Facebook/Meta.",
    "jscodeshift wraps the Recast AST parser and provides a chainable, jQuery-like API for querying and manipulating AST nodes. In a jscodeshift transformer, you begin by finding all JSXElement or JSXOpeningElement nodes matching the target component name. For each matching node, you can query its attributes, extract prop values, compute transformed values, delete deprecated attributes, and insert new structured properties.",
    "Consider an enterprise migration where a monolithic Modal component is evolved into a compound Dialog component. In the legacy version, developers wrote <Modal title='Delete Account' onClose={handleClose}>Are you sure?</Modal>. In the new architecture, accessibility and flexible layout require compound sub-components: <Dialog open={true} onOpenChange={handleClose}><Dialog.Header><Dialog.Title>Delete Account</Dialog.Title></Dialog.Header><Dialog.Body>Are you sure?</Dialog.Body></Dialog>. A jscodeshift script can perform this complex transformation across ten thousand files in under thirty seconds.",
    "By shipping battle-tested jscodeshift codemods via a simple CLI command (such as npx @pinit/codemods v2-dialog-migration src/), the design system team empowers product engineers to upgrade entire massive enterprise codebases with a single command. Automated codemods turn multi-quarter migration nightmares into delightful, coffee-break upgrades that build trust and enthusiasm across the engineering organization.",
    "In addition to transforming JSX attributes, jscodeshift codemods intelligently rewrite import statements at the top of modified files. If a file imported Modal from @pinit/components, the transformer removes the Modal specifier and injects Dialog, ensuring that TypeScript compiler checks pass immediately following transformation. If the file already imported an unrelated Dialog identifier, the codemod safely aliases the new import as PinitDialog to prevent naming collisions.",
    "To ensure complete safety before running codemods across production code, the design system CLI provides a --dry-run and --print flag. Engineers can preview exact git diffs in their terminal before committing changes to disk. Coupled with snapshot testing verification, product squads can execute automated major version migrations across hundreds of source files with absolute peace of mind.",
    "Finally, codemod execution suites feature automated formatting and lint pass hooks. After rewriting the syntax tree, jscodeshift runs the project's local Prettier and ESLint configurations across modified files. This ensures that the generated modern code conforms exactly to the target repository's whitespace, semicolon, and quoting conventions, producing immaculate pull requests that require zero manual touch-ups.",
    "Beyond modifying individual component invocations, enterprise codemods can perform intelligent dependency tree pruning. If an upgraded file no longer requires a legacy helper package or polyfill library, the codemod identifies the orphaned dependency and prompts the developer to run package uninstallation commands. By removing obsolete code artifacts simultaneously with component upgrades, the codemod prevents dependency bloat and keeps bundle sizes minimal across enterprise micro-frontends, maximizing runtime speed and application performance."
]

