    "The only viable path forward in large-scale organizations is Multi-Version Coexistence: engineering the design system so that Version 1 and Version 2 can run simultaneously inside the same browser window, on the same document DOM tree, with zero visual conflicts, zero CSS custom property collisions, and zero JavaScript runtime crashes. If an engineer opens an investment portfolio page where the header runs on Version 2 but an embedded trade blotter widget still runs on Version 1, both versions must render with perfect brand fidelity and visual harmony.",
    "The primary obstacle to coexistence is CSS custom property collision and global stylesheet leakage. If Version 1 defines :root { --pinit-color-primary: #0d6efd; --pinit-spacing-md: 16px; } and Version 2 defines :root { --pinit-color-primary: #6366f1; --pinit-spacing-md: 12px; }, whichever stylesheet loads last will overwrite the variables of the other, destroying the layout of the earlier version. To prevent global collision, modern design systems implement version-scoped CSS namespaces. In Version 2, all CSS variables and class names are prefixed with a major version identifier: --pinit-v2-color-primary, or scoped within isolated container attributes like [data-pinit-version='2'].",
    "Additionally, package bundling tools like npm and pnpm support package aliasing. A consumer application can install both versions simultaneously in package.json using alias syntax: '@pinit-v1/components': 'npm:@pinit/components@1.8.4' and '@pinit/components': '^2.0.0'. This allows product squads to migrate individual screens or components incrementally: new features are written with Version 2 components, while legacy screens continue using Version 1 components without breaking.",
