    "Beyond CSS custom property isolation, coexistence architecture prevents JavaScript runtime collision in shared global spaces. For instance, if Version 1 and Version 2 both register custom web components using customElements.define('pinit-button'), the browser will throw an uncaught DOMException because custom element tag names must be globally unique. Our coexistence engine automatically appends major version suffixes to custom element names: pinit-v1-button and pinit-v2-button, allowing custom elements from multiple major generations to mount on the same page without error.",
    "Furthermore, shared singleton services like theme providers and notification dispatchers are isolated via version-keyed symbols attached to the globalThis object. Version 2 components read from globalThis[Symbol.for('pinit.v2.theme')], ensuring that legacy Version 1 state mutations cannot corrupt modern component trees. This strict isolation guarantees rock-solid stability during gradual enterprise migrations.",
    "Additionally, coexistence harnesses support bridge adapters that translate data between generations. If a modern Version 2 parent container passes theme context to a legacy Version 1 child widget, an automated bridge adapter converts Version 2 semantic tokens back into Version 1 CSS variables dynamically, preserving consistent brand colors across generation boundaries.",
    "Furthermore, coexistence architecture safeguards event bubbling and modal portal stacking contexts. When a Version 1 dropdown is opened inside a Version 2 dialog overlay, the portal manager ensures that z-index hierarchies are unified across library boundaries. An elevation coordinate resolver maps legacy z-index integers to modern design token elevations, guaranteeing that floating menus never render behind modal backdrops regardless of which version rendered the trigger element."
